\alias{lp_problem}
\title{R6 Class for Sparse Linear Programs}
\description{
Collects the constraints of a linear program in long form and hands them to
\code{\link[lpSolve:lp]{lpSolve::lp()}} in triplet form, which keeps memory linear in the number of non-zero
coefficients. Constraints are tagged with the block they belong to, so a program can be
assembled once and solved over a subset of its blocks.
}
\details{
Two tables describe a program.

\code{variables} has one row per decision variable: an \code{id_var} giving its column in the
program, a \code{block} naming the group it belongs to, and whatever further key columns the
caller needs to identify it. Nothing about the meaning of a variable is known here; the
subclass supplies it.

\code{constraints} has one row per non-zero coefficient: \code{block}, \code{id_row}, \code{id_var},
\code{coefficient}, plus the \code{dir} and \code{rhs} of the row it belongs to, which are constant
within an \code{id_row}. Coefficients are summed over duplicated \verb{(id_row, id_var)} pairs and
exact zeroes are dropped, since \code{\link[lpSolve:lp]{lpSolve::lp()}} matches constraint rows to \code{dir}/\code{rhs} by
the order of the row indices it is given, and cannot represent an empty row.

All variables are non-negative: \code{\link[lpSolve:lp]{lpSolve::lp()}} has no notion of variable bounds, so a
quantity that may take either sign has to be split into two variables.

\code{lpSolve} is a suggested dependency, since most of the package does not solve linear
programs. It has to be installed before a program can be constructed.
}
\seealso{
\link{trans_rate_lp}
}
